---
import Journey from '../../layouts/Journey.astro';
import Step from '../../components/journey/Step.astro';
import More from '../../components/journey/More.astro';
import Next from '../../components/journey/Next.astro';
import CopyPrompt from '../../components/site/CopyPrompt.astro';
import { url } from '../../lib/nav';
---
<Journey chapter="grow" first="adds" title="The project grows" description="Every feature adds code and cruft. Clean-ups and refactors are jobs you schedule, too.">
  <Step id="adds" title="Every job adds to the codebase">
    <p>Each finished task grows <a href={url('journey/loop/')}>your project from step 2</a> and lands a commit on main, past <a href={url('journey/check/')}>the check from step 5</a>. As the codebase expands, every new agent has to read more context before making its first edit.</p>
  </Step>
  <Step id="junk" title="And leaves clutter behind">
    <p>Agents also leave behind dead code, unused helpers, and near-duplicates (shown in hatched grey). That clutter slows down every future job—an agent still has to read dead code just to discover it is dead.</p>
  </Step>
  <Step id="clean" title="A clean-up is a job of its own">
    <p>Schedule a dedicated clean-up pass: it reads the project once, strips out the dead code while keeping the tests green before and after, and shrinks what every subsequent job has to read.</p>
    <div class="row">
      <button class="btn" type="button" data-do="clean">Run a clean-up</button>
    </div>
    <More title="A prompt for this">
      <p>Run the tests first—they are what prove a removal broke nothing.</p>
      <CopyPrompt compact title="A clean-up pass">Run the tests and tell me they pass before you change anything. Then find what is unused or duplicated in this project: dead code, copies, files nothing imports, tests for things that are gone. List it, with how you know each item is unused, and wait for me. Then remove one kind at a time and run the tests after each.</CopyPrompt>
    </More>
  </Step>
  <Step id="refactor" title="A refactor changes the structure, not the behaviour">
    <p>When a few files grow oversized, a refactor splits them into smaller, cleaner pieces without changing what the app does. Without tests, a subtle breakage slips straight through to main.</p>
    <div class="pick">
      <button type="button" data-do="tests" data-val="on" data-default aria-pressed="true">With tests</button>
      <button type="button" data-do="tests" data-val="off" aria-pressed="false">Without</button>
    </div>
    <More title="A prompt for this">
      <p>Tests come first because they are the only reliable way to confirm the behaviour stayed the same.</p>
      <CopyPrompt compact title="A refactor with tests first">Before you change anything, run the tests and tell me what they cover in the part you will touch. If something there has no test, write one and show me it passes. Then split the part into smaller files without changing behaviour, running the tests after each move. If a test fails, stop and tell me.</CopyPrompt>
    </More>
  </Step>
  <Step id="rhythm" title="Make maintenance part of the rhythm">
    <p>Watch twenty jobs play out with periodic clean-ups. The longer you put off cleaning up, the more clutter piles up—and the more reading the twentieth job has to wade through.</p>
    <div class="controls">
      <label class="control"><span>A clean-up every <output>5 jobs</output></span>
        <input type="range" min="2" max="11" step="1" value="5" data-bind="every" aria-valuetext="5 jobs" />
      </label>
    </div>
    <Next from="grow" more={[{ href: 'wiki/test/', text: 'Tests', side: true }, { href: 'wiki/review/', text: 'Review', side: true }]} />
  </Step>
  <Fragment slot="foot">
    <p>The codebase growth, clutter accumulation, and context-reading curves above are illustrative examples.</p>
  </Fragment>
</Journey>
